{"model":"kimi-k3","created_at":"2026-09-08T21:51:47.219842754Z","response":"{\"reference_patch\":\"def normalize_record(record):\\n    stage = clean_strings(record)\\n    stage = normalize_authors(stage)\\n    stage = normalize_tags(stage)\\n    result = dict(stage)\\n    authors = result.get(\\\"authors\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(authors, list):\\n        authors = []\\n        result[\\\"authors\\\"] = authors\\n    result[\\\"slug\\\"] = derive_slug(stage)\\n    result[\\\"author_count\\\"] = len(authors)\\n    result[\\\"primary_author\\\"] = authors[0] if authors else None\\n    title = stage.get(\\\"title\\\")\\n    if isinstance(title, str):\\n        result[\\\"title_words\\\"] = len(title.split())\\n    else:\\n        result[\\\"title_words\\\"] = 0\\n    tags = result.get(\\\"tags\\\")\\n    if result.get(\\\"draft\\\") is True:\\n        result[\\\"visibility\\\"] = \\\"internal\\\"\\n    elif isinstance(tags, list) and \\\"embargo\\\" in tags:\\n        result[\\\"visibility\\\"] = \\\"restricted\\\"\\n    else:\\n        result[\\\"visibility\\\"] = \\\"public\\\"\\n    return result\\n\",\"public_checks\":[{\"check_id\":\"staged03-r1-pub-01\",\"symbol\":\"normalize_record\",\"input\":{\"title\":\"  A  B \"},\"expected_values\":[{\"title\":\"A B\",\"authors\":[],\"slug\":\"a-b\",\"author_count\":0,\"primary_author\":null,\"title_words\":2,\"visibility\":\"public\"}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":null,\"source_ids\":[\"m01\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"functionality\",\"rationale\":\"m01 explicitly requires {'title': '  A  B '} to end with title_words 2, counting whitespace-separated words of the cleaned title. m05 requires preserving the pipeline, so clean_strings first collapses the title to 'A B', derive_slug yields 'a-b', authors defaults to [] with count 0 and no other visibility branch applies.\"},{\"check_id\":\"staged03-r1-pub-02\",\"symbol\":\"normalize_record\",\"input\":{},\"expected_values\":[{\"authors\":[],\"slug\":\"untitled\",\"author_count\":0,\"primary_author\":null,\"title_words\":0,\"visibility\":\"public\"}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":null,\"source_ids\":[\"m04\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"functionality\",\"rationale\":\"m04 locks the empty-record boundary: slug 'untitled' from derive_slug on the cleaned record, authors [], author_count 0, title_words 0 (missing title per m01), primary_author None (m02), and visibility 'public' since neither draft nor embargo applies (m03).\"},{\"check_id\":\"staged03-r1-pub-03\",\"symbol\":\"normalize_record\",\"input\":{\"title\":\"Paper\",\"authors\":[\"Jane Doe\",\"jane doe\",\"Jane Doe\"],\"tags\":[\"EMBARGO\"]},\"expected_values\":[{\"title\":\"Paper\",\"authors\":[\"Jane Doe\",\"jane doe\"],\"tags\":[\"embargo\"],\"slug\":\"paper\",\"author_count\":2,\"primary_author\":\"Jane Doe\",\"title_words\":1,\"visibility\":\"restricted\"}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":null,\"source_ids\":[\"m02\",\"m03\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"functionality\",\"rationale\":\"m02 keeps author case untouched with exact-match dedup, so 'Jane Doe' and 'jane doe' both survive and primary_author is 'Jane Doe' with author_count 2. m03 adds only the embargo branch and leaves tag cleaning unchanged, so 'EMBARGO' normalizes to 'embargo' and visibility becomes 'restricted'; draft is absent, so the internal branch is not triggered.\"},{\"check_id\":\"staged03-r1-pub-04\",\"symbol\":\"normalize_record\",\"input\":{\"title\":\"Draft Doc\",\"draft\":true,\"tags\":[\"embargo\"]},\"expected_values\":[{\"title\":\"Draft Doc\",\"draft\":true,\"tags\":[\"embargo\"],\"authors\":[],\"slug\":\"draft-doc\",\"author_count\":0,\"primary_author\":null,\"title_words\":2,\"visibility\":\"internal\"}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":null,\"source_ids\":[\"m03\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"functionality\",\"rationale\":\"m03 states draft records still win with 'internal' even when embargoed, so the draft branch is checked first and the embargo tag, though present after normalization, does not change the outcome; the public default is overridden only by these two branches.\"}],\"private_checks\":[{\"check_id\":\"staged03-r1-prv-01\",\"symbol\":\"normalize_record\",\"input\":{\"title\":42},\"expected_values\":[{\"title\":42,\"authors\":[],\"slug\":\"untitled\",\"author_count\":0,\"primary_author\":null,\"title_words\":0,\"visibility\":\"public\"}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":null,\"source_ids\":[\"m01\",\"m02\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"functionality\",\"rationale\":\"m01 sets title_words to 0 when the title is not a string; the preserved derive_slug fallback (kept per m05) also bases the slug on 'untitled' for a non-string title, while the non-string title value itself passes through clean_strings untouched.\"},{\"check_id\":\"staged03-r1-prv-02\",\"symbol\":\"normalize_record\",\"input\":{\"title\":\"Solo\",\"authors\":\"  Alice   Smith  \",\"tags\":[\"news\",\"embargo\",\"news\"],\"draft\":false},\"expected_values\":[{\"title\":\"Solo\",\"authors\":[\"Alice Smith\"],\"tags\":[\"news\",\"embargo\"],\"draft\":false,\"slug\":\"solo\",\"author_count\":1,\"primary_author\":\"Alice Smith\",\"title_words\":1,\"visibility\":\"restricted\"}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":null,\"source_ids\":[\"m01\",\"m02\",\"m03\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"functionality\",\"rationale\":\"Interaction case: the preserved pipeline (m01, m05) wraps a single string author and whitespace-cleans it to 'Alice Smith', making primary_author 'Alice Smith' with count 1 (m02). Tag normalization dedups 'news' and keeps 'embargo'; draft is false rather than true, so only the embargo branch fires and visibility is 'restricted' (m03).\"},{\"check_id\":\"staged03-r1-prv-03\",\"symbol\":\"normalize_record\",\"input\":{\"title\":\"Cooking\",\"edition\":3},\"expected_values\":[{\"title\":\"Cooking\",\"edition\":3,\"authors\":[],\"slug\":\"cooking-ed3\",\"author_count\":0,\"primary_author\":null,\"title_words\":1,\"visibility\":\"public\"}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":1,\"source_ids\":[\"m01\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"retained\",\"rationale\":\"The initial derive_slug appends '-ed3' for integer edition \u003e 1, and m05 requires preserving all still-applicable behavior during this round's normalize_record update; nothing in m01-m04 retires the edition suffix, so it holds here and the case is bounded to round 1 so later derive_slug changes cannot conflict.\"},{\"check_id\":\"staged03-r1-prv-04\",\"symbol\":\"normalize_record\",\"input\":{\"title\":\"X\",\"tags\":[\"A_B\",\"a b\",\"a-b\"]},\"expected_values\":[{\"title\":\"X\",\"tags\":[\"a-b\"],\"authors\":[],\"slug\":\"x\",\"author_count\":0,\"primary_author\":null,\"title_words\":1,\"visibility\":\"public\"}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":null,\"source_ids\":[\"m01\",\"m03\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"retained\",\"rationale\":\"The initial normalize_tags lowercases, converts underscores to spaces, hyphenates and dedups, collapsing all three variants to 'a-b'; m03 explicitly leaves tag cleaning untouched and m01/m05 keep the pipeline order, so this retained cleaning behavior is verified through normalize_record with no embargo tag present, leaving the public default.\"},{\"check_id\":\"staged03-r1-prv-05\",\"symbol\":\"normalize_record\",\"input\":{\"authors\":null},\"expected_values\":[{\"authors\":[],\"slug\":\"untitled\",\"author_count\":0,\"primary_author\":null,\"title_words\":0,\"visibility\":\"public\"}],\"active_from_round\":1,\"active_through_round\":null,\"source_ids\":[\"m02\",\"m04\",\"m05\"],\"behavior\":\"functionality\",\"rationale\":\"m02 requires the authors key always present as a list even when the input had none, which covers an explicit None value: it becomes [], so author_count is 0 and primary_author None; with no title, m01 and the preserved slug fallback give title_words 0 and 'untitled', and no visibility branch applies.\"}]}","thinking":"Let me carefully parse this task.\n\nWe have a project \"staged-03\", round 1. I need to author the round 1 reference patch and behavioral checks.\n\nThe initial module has functions: clean_strings, normalize_authors, normalize_tags, derive_slug, normalize_record.\n\nRound 1 target: normalize_record (module.py). Messages m01..m05 visible (four source messages m01-m04 plus request m05).\n\nRequirements from m01-m04:\n\nm01: Keep pipeline shape: clean_strings, normalize_authors, normalize_tags, then slug, author_count, visibility, all on a fresh dict. Add `title_words` field: number of whitespace-separated words in the cleaned title, or 0 when the title is missing or not a string. {'title': ' A B '} must end with title_words 2.\n\nm02: Add `primary_author`: first entry of normalized authors list, or None when empty. author_count stays the length of the same list. The authors key must always be present in the output as a list, even when the input had none. Author case stays untouched (don't lowercase author names).\n\nm03: New visibility branch: after tag normalization, when 'embargo' is among the tags, visibility becomes 'restricted'. Draft records still win with 'internal' even if embargoed; everything else 'public'.\n\nm04: normalize_record({}) must yield slug 'untitled', authors [], author_count 0, title_words 0, primary_author None, visibility 'public', and leave input untouched. Slug must come from derive_slug called on the cleaned record; pipeline order unchanged.\n\nSo the reference patch: a complete replacement `normalize_record` function.\n\nNote: the authors key always present as a list. Currently normalize_authors returns fixed unchanged if authors is None. So in normalize_record, we need result[\"authors\"] to be a list. The pipeline: stage = clean_strings(record), stage = normalize_authors(stage), stage = normalize_tags(stage), result = dict(stage). Then we need authors list: authors = result.get(\"authors\") — if not a list? Well, normalize_authors only converts if authors key present (str -\u003e list, list -\u003e cleaned). If authors was None, the key remains None? Actually normalize_authors: authors = fixed.get(\"authors\"); if authors is None: return fixed — so if the key is absent, get returns None, returns fixed without the key. If the key exists with value None, same — returns fixed with \"authors\": None still in dict? Wait fixed = dict(record), so if record had \"authors\": None, fixed has \"authors\": None and it returns without change, so None stays.\n\nFor round 1, normalize_record must ensure authors is a list. Cases: authors missing -\u003e []; authors None -\u003e []; authors not a str/list (e.g., int)? normalize_authors would leave it as is (not a list). Should normalize_record coerce to []? The spec says \"the authors key is always present in the output as a list\". To be safe, if after pipeline result[\"authors\"] is not a list, set to []. That's a reasonable interpretation. But careful: normalize_authors handles str -\u003e [str] and list -\u003e cleaned list. Non-list, non-str (e.g. int) — spec doesn't say; simplest to coerce non-list to []. Let me do:\n\n```python\ndef normalize_record(record):\n    stage = clean_strings(record)\n    stage = normalize_authors(stage)\n    stage = normalize_tags(stage)\n    result = dict(stage)\n    authors = result.get(\"authors\")\n    if not isinstance(authors, list):\n        authors = []\n        result[\"authors\"] = authors\n    result[\"slug\"] = derive_slug(stage)\n    result[\"author_count\"] = len(authors)\n    result[\"primary_author\"] = authors[0] if authors else None\n    title = result.get(\"title\")\n    if isinstance(title, str):\n        result[\"title_words\"] = len(title.split())\n    else:\n        result[\"title_words\"] = 0\n    if result.get(\"draft\") is True:\n        result[\"visibility\"] = \"internal\"\n    elif \"embargo\" in result.get(\"tags\", []):\n        result[\"visibility\"] = \"restricted\"\n    else:\n        result[\"visibility\"] = \"public\"\n    return result\n```\n\nWait — \"title_words\" = number of whitespace-separated words in the cleaned title. Cleaned title is stage[\"title\"] after clean_strings. Note clean_strings normalizes whitespace: \" \".join(value.split()). So the number of words is the same before/after cleaning. But use cleaned: title = stage.get(\"title\"). Actually result = dict(stage), so result[\"title\"] is the cleaned one. Use stage or result, same.\n\nEmbargo check: \"after tag normalization, when 'embargo' is among the tags\". tags in result after normalize_tags is a list (if input was list). If tags missing/not a list, result.get(\"tags\", []) might be a non-list... e.g. record has \"tags\": \"embargo\"? normalize_tags returns fixed unchanged since not a list, so result[\"tags\"] = \"embargo\" (string). Then \"embargo\" in \"embargo\" is True (substring check on string!). Hmm. That would make visibility restricted. Is that intended? The spec says \"when 'embargo' is among the tags\" — tags should be a list. Edge case where tags is a string might be weird. Safer: tags = result.get(\"tags\"); if not isinstance(tags, list): tags = []; then check membership. That avoids substring weirdness. Let me do:\n\n```python\n    tags = result.get(\"tags\")\n    if not isinstance(tags, list):\n        tags = []\n```\n\nThen visibility:\n\n```python\n    if result.get(\"draft\") is True:\n        result[\"visibility\"] = \"internal\"\n    elif \"embargo\" in tags:\n        result[\"visibility\"] = \"restricted\"\n    else:\n        result[\"visibility\"] = \"public\"\n```\n\nOrder: draft wins over embargo. Good.\n\nSlug: \"The slug must still come from derive_slug called on the cleaned record\" — derive_slug(stage) where stage is after the three helpers. result = dict(stage) so same content. I'll pass stage.\n\nNote: derive_slug at round 1 is still the initial version with edition handling (retired only in round 2). So normalize_record({'title': 'Cooking', 'edition': 3}) at round 1 would give slug 'cooking-ed3'. That's still-applicable behavior in round 1. Good — don't anticipate round 2.\n\nToken budget: replacement within 1024 tokens — easily fine.\n\nNow checks.\n\nRound 1 needs:\n- public_checks: checks introduced this round (public visible). Fields: In this round packet, per the contract: \"Every public/private check has exact fields: {check_id, symbol, input, expected_values, active_from_round, active_through_round, source_ids, behavior, rationale}.\" The runtime projection strips to 4 fields later, but we author full fields. The output format: {\"reference_patch\": string, \"public_checks\": [...], \"private_checks\": [...]}.\n\nCheck IDs unique across project. Intro = current round 1; expiry inclusive or null. Since we can see future rounds: round 2 changes derive_slug (edition retired, version added, 8-word cap). Round 1 checks on normalize_record that depend on slug behavior might conflict with round 2/3 if they remain active. E.g. a normalize_record check with input {'title': 'Cooking', 'edition': 3} expecting slug 'cooking-ed3' would be wrong in round 2. So such a check must expire at round 1 (active_through_round: 1). Or avoid slug expectations sensitive to future changes.\n\nRound 3: kind namespacing, token length caps, 60-char cap.\n\nLet me plan checks:\n\nPublic checks (visible to workers at introduction; should be meaningful but compact; maybe 3-4):\n\np1: normalize_record({'title': '  A  B '}) → title_words 2, authors [], author_count 0, primary_author None, slug 'a-b', visibility 'public'. Wait — slug for title 'A B' cleaned to 'A B' → derive_slug lowercases: 'a-b'. Input raw title is \"  A  B \" — input to normalize_record. Cleaned title \"A B\". slug 'a-b'. Expected output full dict? expected_values are full outputs (strict JSON equality on output). normalize_record returns full dict. So expected: {\"title\": \"A B\", \"authors\": [], \"slug\": \"a-b\", \"author_count\": 0, \"primary_author\": None, \"title_words\": 2, \"visibility\": \"public\"}. This check remains valid across rounds (no edition/version/kind). active_through_round: null.\n\np2: embargo + draft interaction: {'title': 'Doc', 'tags': ['Embargo'], 'draft': True} → after normalize_tags, 'embargo' present; draft wins → internal. Expected: title 'Doc', tags ['embargo'], authors [], slug 'doc', author_count 0, primary_author None, title_words 1, visibility 'internal'. Active through null (valid all rounds).\n\np3: embargo restricted: {'title': 'Paper', 'authors': '  Jane   Doe ', 'tags': ['embargo'] } → authors ['Jane Doe'], author_count 1, primary_author 'Jane Doe', visibility 'restricted', slug 'paper', title_words 1. Active null. Also tests case preservation of authors.\n\np4 maybe: duplicate authors/tags dedup + edition? Edition slug depends on round. Let's do a public check with edition expiring at round 1 to cover the still-applicable edition behavior through normalize_record: {'title': 'Cooking', 'edition': 3} → slug 'cooking-ed3' in round 1, expired after round 1. Hmm but is that wise to include as public check that expires? It's allowed: active_from_round 1, active_through_round 1. Behavior \"retained\" (from initial scaffold). Round 2 will add its own retirement checks (round 2 packet authored separately... wait, we author ALL rounds? No — \"Current round (integer): 1\" and \"Author exactly one round's reference patch and behavioral checks\". For rounds1 and2 output exactly those keys. We output only round 1's packet. We only see the schedule to set expiry intervals.\n\nSo I need to ensure round 1 checks' expiry intervals are consistent with future changes. Round 2 will retire edition in derive_slug; any round-1 check whose expected output depends on edition suffix must expire at round 1.\n\nPrivate checks: at least 2, at least one new functionality. Also need at least two active private cases for retained behavior in round 1 (can derive from initial module behavior + source instructions).\n\nLet me design checks:\n\nPublic (round 1):\n1. c_pub_01: title_words basic — {'title': '  A  B '} → expected as above. source_ids ['m01','m05']. behavior functionality. Active null.\n\nHmm wait, slug 'a-b' stays same in rounds 2 and 3 (no kind/version/edition; 8-word cap doesn't affect; 60-char cap fine). Good.\n\n2. c_pub_02: empty dict {} → full defaults per m04: {\"authors\": [], \"slug\": \"untitled\", \"author_count\": 0, \"primary_author\": None, \"title_words\": 0, \"visibility\": \"public\"}. All rounds valid. source_ids ['m04','m05']. functionality.\n\nWait — round 3: derive_slug({'...'}) with kind missing → 'untitled'. Yes unchanged. Valid through 3.\n\n3. c_pub_03: embargo restricted with authors: {'title': 'Paper', 'authors': ['Jane Doe', 'jane doe', 'Jane Doe'], 'tags': ['EMBARGO']} → authors dedup case-sensitively: ['Jane Doe', 'jane doe'] (case untouched, dedup exact). author_count 2, primary_author 'Jane Doe'. tags ['embargo']. visibility 'restricted'. slug 'paper'. title_words 1. source_ids ['m02','m03','m05']. functionality. Valid all rounds.\n\nHmm, is dedup case-sensitive? normalize_authors: \"if name and name not in cleaned\" — exact match. 'jane doe' != 'Jane Doe'. And m02 explicitly says don't lowercase authors. Good.\n\n4. c_pub_04: draft beats embargo: {'title': 'Draft Doc', 'draft': True, 'tags': ['embargo']} → visibility 'internal', slug 'draft-doc', title_words 2, authors [], etc. source_ids ['m03','m05']. functionality.\n\nMaybe also a public retained check on edition+slug expiring at 1? Public checks with expiry... allowed? active_through_round is part of every check. Round-1-only public checks are fine. But public checks accompany work when active; expiring at 1 means only relevant round 1. Let me instead keep public checks future-proof and put edition-related ones in private with expiry 1, since retirement testing belongs to round 2 (private retirement cases introduced round 2 by round-2 author). Actually round 1 could include a private retained case covering edition suffix behavior (still applicable in round 1) with active_through_round 1. That documents round-1 behavior and expires before round 2's change. That seems good and avoids conflicting with future.\n\nPrivate checks round 1 (need ≥2, ≥1 functionality; also ≥2 retained active at round 1):\n\nprivate_01 (functionality): authors missing entirely → [] and primary_author None, plus title_words non-string title: {'title': 42} → title_words 0, slug from derive_slug: title not str → 'untitled'. Wait title 42, clean_strings leaves it. derive_slug: not isinstance(title, str) → 'untitled'. expected: {\"title\": 42, \"authors\": [], \"slug\": \"untitled\", \"author_count\": 0, \"primary_author\": None, \"title_words\": 0, \"visibility\": \"public\"}. source_ids m01, m02, m05. Active null. Also covers boundary non-string title.\n\nprivate_02 (functionality / interaction): authors given as single string + embargo + draft False: {'title': 'Solo', 'authors': '  Alice   Smith  ', 'tags': ['news', 'embargo', 'news'], 'draft': False} → authors ['Alice Smith'], author_count 1, primary_author 'Alice Smith', tags ['news', 'embargo'] (dedup), visibility restricted. draft False → not internal. slug 'solo'. title_words 1. source_ids m01-m03, m05. Active null.\n\nprivate_03 (retained, expiry 1): edition still applies in round 1 through normalize_record: {'title': 'Cooking', 'edition': 3} → expected {\"title\": \"Cooking\", \"edition\": 3, \"authors\": [], \"slug\": \"cooking-ed3\", \"author_count\": 0, \"primary_author\": None, \"title_words\": 1, \"visibility\": \"public\"}. active_from 1, through 1. behavior retained. source_ids: m04/m05 + ... The retained rule comes from initial module + m01 \"preserving all still-applicable behavior\" / m05 request text \"preserving all still-applicable behavior\". Source_ids must be nonempty unique IDs visible at introduction: m05 says \"preserving all still-applicable behavior\" — that justifies retained. Use ['m05'] or ['m01','m05']. m01 says keep current pipeline shape... the edition behavior is from initial scaffold preserved by \"preserving all still-applicable behavior\" in m05. I'll cite ['m05'].\n\nWait, but round 2 will need at least two active private retirement cases — those are introduced by the round-2 packet (not ours). We only author round 1. Fine.\n\nprivate_04 (retained): dedup of tags and non-mutation: {'title': 'X', 'tags': ['A_B', 'a b', 'a-b']} → normalize_tags: 'A_B' → lower 'a_b' replace '_'→' ' 'a b' → strip → split join '-' → 'a-b'. 'a b' → 'a-b' dup. 'a-b' → 'a-b' dup. tags ['a-b']. visibility public. slug 'x'. authors []. title_words 1. expected: {\"title\": \"X\", \"tags\": [\"a-b\"], \"authors\": [], \"slug\": \"x\", \"author_count\": 0, \"primary_author\": None, \"title_words\": 1, \"visibility\": \"public\"}. source_ids ['m01','m05'] (pipeline unchanged). retained. Active null.\n\nAlso private_05 (retained boundary): embargo-like tag cleaning: tag 'Embargo ' with underscore? e.g. tags [' embargo'] → cleaned 'embargo' → restricted. That's functionality of m03 interaction with cleaning. Let me instead ensure boundary: tags not a list, e.g. {'title': 'T', 'tags': 'embargo'} → tags stays 'embargo' string in output (normalize_tags ignores non-list). visibility: \"embargo among the tags\" — tags not a list; spec says after tag normalization when embargo among the tags. With string tags, not a list of tags; my reference coerces to [] for the check so visibility 'public'. Hmm, is that justified? m03 says \"when 'embargo' is among the tags\" — tags as normalized list. Ambiguous for non-list tags. This check might be over-forcing a permitted choice. Risky: independent review checks \"whether a permitted choice is incorrectly forced\". The spec doesn't define non-list tags for visibility. I'd better avoid that edge in checks, OR design reference to treat string tags... Hmm. Actually safer reference: check `\"embargo\" in tags` where tags is the result value only if list else skip. My choice is defensible but I shouldn't lock it with a check since it's unspecified. Avoid such a check.\n\nBut wait, must be careful: reference `\"embargo\" in result.get(\"tags\", [])` if tags is a string would do substring matching — treat 'embargo' in 'disembargo' etc. I'll coerce non-list to no-embargo. That's a safe interpretation; not checked.\n\nAlso private check: authors None explicitly → []. {'authors': None, 'title': 'T'} → authors []. Good functionality for m02 (\"even when the input had none\"). Include private_05: {'authors': None} → {\"authors\": [], \"slug\": \"untitled\", \"author_count\": 0, \"primary_author\": None, \"title_words\": 0, \"visibility\": \"public\"}. Hmm input {'authors': None} → clean_strings no-op; normalize_authors: authors None → return fixed with 'authors': None present. Then reference coerces to []. Good. Active null.\n\nCount private checks: need at least 2 with ≥1 functionality; at least 2 active retained. I have functionality: p01 (title 42), p02 (string author + embargo), p05 (authors None). Retained: p03 (edition, expires 1), p04 (tag normalization dedup). That's 2 retained active in round 1. Good.\n\nPublic checks count: contract doesn't set a minimum for public? \"Add compact, meaningful public and private cases\". Each round: \"at least two private cases\". Public minimum not stated explicitly; initial rounds list public_checks per round. I'll include 4 public.\n\nNow check uniqueness of IDs across project: use ids like \"r1-pub-1\" etc. Since rounds 2-3 authored later by others(?) Actually same assembler—IDs must be unique across project; later round packets will introduce their own. Choose distinctive prefix \"staged03-r1-...\" to avoid collisions.\n\nactive_from_round = 1 for all. active_through_round null except edition one = 1.\n\nCheck expected values: full output dicts. Note dict key order irrelevant in JSON equality presumably (\"strict JSON equality\"). Provide expected_values as list with single dict each.\n\nNow verify each expected output manually:\n\nReference:\n\n```python\ndef normalize_record(record):\n    stage = clean_strings(record)\n    stage = normalize_authors(stage)\n    stage = normalize_tags(stage)\n    result = dict(stage)\n    authors = result.get(\"authors\")\n    if not isinstance(authors, list):\n        authors = []\n        result[\"authors\"] = authors\n    result[\"slug\"] = derive_slug(stage)\n    result[\"author_count\"] = len(authors)\n    if authors:\n        result[\"primary_author\"] = authors[0]\n    else:\n        result[\"primary_author\"] = None\n    title = stage.get(\"title\")\n    if isinstance(title, str):\n        result[\"title_words\"] = len(title.split())\n    else:\n        result[\"title_words\"] = 0\n    tags = result.get(\"tags\")\n    if result.get(\"draft\") is True:\n        result[\"visibility\"] = \"internal\"\n    elif isinstance(tags, list) and \"embargo\" in tags:\n        result[\"visibility\"] = \"restricted\"\n    else:\n        result[\"visibility\"] = \"public\"\n    return result\n```\n\ntitle_words: m01 says \"number of whitespace-separated words in the cleaned title\". Cleaned title = after clean_strings. Using stage.get(\"title\"). Yes.\n\nCheck c-pub-1: input {\"title\": \"  A  B \"}.\nclean_strings: title → \"A B\". authors absent. tags absent. slug derive_slug: title \"A B\" → lower split ['a','b'] → 'a-b'. No edition → 'a-b'. authors [] ; author_count 0; primary None; title_words 2; visibility public. Output: {\"title\": \"A B\", \"authors\": [], \"slug\": \"a-b\", \"author_count\": 0, \"primary_author\": None, \"title_words\": 2, \"visibility\": \"public\"}. ✔ m01 example satisfied.\n\nRounds 2-3: derive_slug {'title': 'A B'} → 'a-b' still (cap 8 fine; no version/kind). ✔ stays valid.\n\nc-pub-2: {} → clean_strings {} ; normalize_authors returns {} (authors None→return fixed, no key); normalize_tags {}. result {}. authors → []. slug derive_slug({}) → title None → 'untitled'. title_words: stage.get('title') None